> **SELF-EXCLUSION (`.md.excluded`).** "Owl Alpha" is **not a distinct model** — it is a **confirmed stealth preview alias** for Meituan's **LongCat-2.0-Preview**, and the endpoint has been **retired (OpenRouter `openrouter.ai/owl-alpha` → 404)**. After a genuine research pass there is **no owl-alpha-branded model card, no weights, and zero independently verifiable benchmarks attributable to an artifact named "Owl Alpha."** Its identity was resolved **by the vendor's own first-party channel**: **Meituan confirmed on 2026-06-30**, on its own blog and the **@Meituan_LongCat X account**, that Owl Alpha was the stealth preview of **LongCat-2.0-Preview (a 1.6T-parameter / 48B-active MoE)**, independently corroborated by **Decrypt (2026-07-01)** (this is row 13 of Digital Applied's dated census of OpenRouter stealth listings, as of 2026-08-22). Because the GA sibling **`longcat-2.0` is separately queued and already scored in this dataset (see `../longcat-2.0/`, and this run's report b3 → Overall 72)**, and the *only* benchmark table that exists under the "LongCat 2.0/2.5" name is **LongCat-2.0's own model card**, **scoring `owl-alpha` would be a reattribution + double-count of LongCat-2.0's numbers** — explicitly forbidden by `RULES.md` / `model-report-TEMPLATE.md`. `pnpm sync` auto-quarantines evidence-free files. **No scores are assigned; no numbers invented.** Same alias/double-count pattern applied to [`../longcat-2.5-preview/`](../longcat-2.5-preview/) and, for stealth aliases, [`../omen-alpha/`](../omen-alpha/)/[`../ox-alpha/`](../ox-alpha/).

## Model card (alias — resolves to LongCat-2.0-Preview; no owl-alpha-specific first-party artifact)

- **Name:** Owl Alpha (a.k.a. `openrouter/owl-alpha`) — **revealed identity: Meituan LongCat-2.0-Preview**
- **Short description:** An anonymous, free **OpenRouter stealth listing** (codename) that ran from **2026-04-28** before the real model shipped. It was **not** a standalone product: Meituan confirmed it was the **stealth preview of LongCat-2.0**, the same 1.6T/48B-active MoE reported in `../longcat-2.0/`. The curated meta describes it accurately as a "**retired free OpenRouter alias … the endpoint now 404s, no vendor and no public scores**," and self-flags "**the folder's findings are a self-exclusion**."
- **Provider / access:** **None remaining** — `openrouter.ai/owl-alpha` returns **404** (retired out of the live catalog). No Artificial Analysis page, no Vals entry, no HF repo exist under the "Owl Alpha" name (AA/HF index the LongCat-2.0 / LongCat-Flash artifacts instead).
- **Release / knowledge:** Listed **2026-04-28**; revealed **2026-06-30** (a 63-day gap — the longest reveal in the stealth census, and the one reveal a watcher only monitoring OpenRouter's own channels would have missed, since it came from **Meituan**, not OpenRouter).
- **IDs:** `owl-alpha` (vendorless OpenRouter alias, retired) → maps to **`meituan-longcat/LongCat-2.0(-Preview)`**.
- **Context window:** **1,048,576 (~1.05M)** per the OpenRouter model-page/API record (third-party listing figure — matches LongCat-2.0's ~1M class; not an owl-alpha-specific spec to independently verify).
- **Modalities:** Reported text in/out at the alias layer; the underlying LongCat-2.0 lineage is a **text** MoE (image-input claims belong to the *Preview/2.5* reseller pages, not to any owl-alpha card). **Unresolved at the alias level** — no artifact to score.
- **Pricing:** **Free during the alias window** ("Yes; pricing not published"); **$0 / no live route remains (404).** Data terms at listing: "*Prompts and completions may be logged by the provider and used to improve the model*" (training-in-scope, default stealth terms).

### Raw benchmarks found

> **None attributable to "Owl Alpha."** No neutral harness (Artificial Analysis / Vals / Epoch / METR / swebench.com / tbench.ai / LMArena) ever evaluated an artifact under that name; the alias carries no model card or tech report of its own. The only benchmark table in the LongCat-2.0 family is **LongCat-2.0's HF model card** (Terminal-Bench 2.1 70.8, SWE-bench Pro 59.5, SWE-bench Multilingual 77.3, GPQA-diamond 88.9, BrowseComp 79.9, IFEval 90.0, etc.) — **already credited to `../longcat-2.0/` (b3)**. Importing it here is a **double-count**.

- **Identity evidence (first-party):** Digital Applied stealth-model census, row 13 — "**Owl Alpha … Turned out to be: Meituan LongCat-2.0-Preview, 1.6T-parameter / 48B-active MoE … Reveal mechanism: Meituan's own blog and @Meituan_LongCat X account; corroborated by Decrypt … 2026-06-30 … Evidence tier: Verified.**" The census records that "**every reveal came from a first-party channel, never from fingerprinting**," so this identity is settled to the highest tier.
- **Endpoint / artifact status:** OpenRouter `owl-alpha` page **404** (fetch attempted this pass); owl-alpha absent from the live catalog. Confirms meta's "no live route remains … findings are a self-exclusion."
- **Cross-check on the "Owl/Ox" confusion:** the alias-family must not be conflated — **Owl Alpha = LongCat-2.0-Preview (Meituan, VERIFIED 2026-06-30)**, whereas **Ox Alpha = GLM-5.3-Flash (Z.ai, VERIFIED 2026-08-26)** and **Omen Alpha = an unresolved GLM-lineage stealth coding alias (b20)**. Each resolves to a *different* underlying vendor; none of them is a distinct benchmarked artifact in its own right.

> Provenance / conclusion: **a real, confirmed stealth alias that maps to a separately-queued model (LongCat-2.0) and now 404s — with zero owl-alpha-specific public benchmarks.** All prongs of the self-exclusion rule are met: no distinct shipped artifact (it is LongCat-2.0-Preview), no independently verifiable evaluation under the Owl Alpha name, and a high reattribution/double-count risk against `../longcat-2.0/`. The curated meta's own tradeoff concurs verbatim: "*No vendor, no weights, no scores and no endpoint - the folder's findings are a self-exclusion.*"
> Recommended curator resolution: **keep `owl-alpha` parked / unscored** and route its capability story to **[`../longcat-2.0/`](../longcat-2.0/)** (which owns the model-card table). **Only if** a neutral harness ever evaluates an `owl-alpha`/LongCat-2.0-Preview id *directly* with its own first-party numbers would a scored `Qwen_3.8_Max.md` be appropriate — and even then it must **not** reimport LongCat-2.0's table.

